<main id="main"><section class="research-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Mechanical Design · Work in Progress</p><h1>Y-Zipper Mechanism</h1><p class="subtitle">Retractable Blade Concept</p><p class="hero-intro">Exploring how three flexible strips can combine into a rigid beam for a compact retractable mechanism.</p><div class="research-meta"><span>August 2026 – Present</span><span>Personal engineering project</span></div><p class="status">In progress · Updated November 2026</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#contributions">My work ↓</a><a class="button" href="#next">Next steps ↓</a></div></div><aside class="focus-panel"><p class="eyebrow">Design Question</p><h2>Flexible strips, rigid beam</h2><p>How can strip geometry, convergence, and storage work together in a compact retractable assembly?</p></aside></section>
<section class="section dark" id="contributions"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>My Work So Far</h2></div><p>Current scope and contributions.</p></div><div class="contribution-list"><div><span>01</span><p>3D printed and manually evaluated existing Y-Zipper strips and the converger/slider mechanism, observing zipping and unzipping behavior and qualitatively assessing assembled-beam stiffness.</p></div><div><span>02</span><p>Investigated the supplied Rhino/Grasshopper workflow and explored changes to strip geometry for a compact retractable application.</p></div><div><span>03</span><p>Estimated spool diameter, usable blade length relative to handle length, and strip-storage volume to assess packaging feasibility.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section split" id="next"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>Next Development Steps</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>Further work includes refining the strip geometry, evaluating bend radius and feed force, and developing the storage and actuation arrangement. Motorized retraction and a completed integrated prototype remain development goals.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Project scope</strong><p>This project adapts an existing publicly available three-strip Y-Zipper mechanism. The original mechanism and baseline geometry are not my invention; my work focuses on fabrication, evaluation, packaging, and adaptation.</p></div></div></section>
